{# Rendering rules shared by more than one DNOS template.

   These live in one file rather than being written twice because the two callers are one bug
   apart: initial/dnos.j2 decides what address a loopback actually gets, and bgp/dnos.j2 has to
   originate exactly that -- so a rule that drifts between them produces a lab whose BGP
   advertises a prefix that was never configured, and says nothing about it. #}

{#  DNOS accepts an IPv6 address on a loopback ONLY as a /128:
      "Loopback interface IPv6 address on lo0 can be configured only with /128"
    netlab plans its loopback pool as a /64, so the address is coerced on the way out. Callers
    must apply this to EVERY loopback-type interface, not just the system loopback: an
    additional loopback is a loopback too. #}
{% macro loopback_ipv6(addr) -%}
{{ addr|ipaddr('address') }}/128
{%- endmacro %}

{#  The BGP counterpart of the same rule. "network" is an EXACT-MATCH origination -- DNOS
    advertises the prefix only while a route for exactly that prefix is in the table -- so
    originating netlab's planned /64 for a loopback advertises nothing at all, silently: the
    prefix sits in the local BGP table unadvertised and un-best, with no error anywhere.

    Given one entry from bgp.advertise, returns the prefix to originate: the /128 that
    initial/dnos.j2 configured if this prefix is a loopback's, and otherwise exactly what netlab
    asked for. Keyed on interface TYPE, so it covers additional loopbacks as well. #}
{% macro origination(pfx, af, ifs) -%}
{% set _lb = [] -%}
{% if af == 'ipv6' -%}
{%   for l in ifs
       if l.type|default('') == 'loopback' and l.ipv6 is defined and l.ipv6 is not sameas true
          and l.ipv6|ipaddr('subnet') == pfx -%}
{%     set _ = _lb.append(l.ipv6) -%}
{%   endfor -%}
{% endif -%}
{{ loopback_ipv6(_lb[0]) if _lb else pfx }}
{%- endmacro %}
